---
import type { BlogCategory } from '@/data/blog';

interface Props {
  category: BlogCategory;
}

const { category } = Astro.props;
---

{
  category === 'Getting Approved' && (
    <>
      <p>
        Getting funded in 24–48 hours after approval is mostly about the file you send, not the pitch. Underwriters
        read deposits, time in business, and whether the account can support a payment before they talk product. These
        guides cover what to gather before you apply and how those bank statements are read, so a specialist is not
        waiting on missing documents.
      </p>
      <p>
        When the file is ready, <a href="/apply" class="link">start an application</a>. A specialist reviews it
        promptly. Approval is never guaranteed. If personal or business credit is standing in the way of a term
        facility, <a href="/resources/credit-repair-loan-prep" class="link">Credit Repair &amp; Loan Prep</a> is the
        resource for bureau work and a free consultation. If you want a payment sketch first, run the{' '}
        <a href="/resources/loan-calculator" class="link">loan calculator</a>, then apply with a number in mind.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use these articles to show up prepared. They do not replace a conversation with a specialist, and every file
        is still reviewed individually.
      </p>
    </>
  )
}
{
  category === 'Compare Options' && (
    <>
      <p>
        Choosing a product is easier when you can see how they differ on speed, repayment, and what underwriting
        actually looks at. Merchant cash advances buy future receivables. Term loans deliver a lump sum on a schedule.
        Invoice factoring sells outstanding B2B invoices. The articles in this category compare those structures in
        plain English so you can match the tool to the job.
      </p>
      <p>
        For the full lineup, including amounts and terms, see the{' '}
        <a href="/services#compare-heading" class="link">compare table on Services</a>. Then read the product pages
        for <a href="/services/merchant-cash-advance" class="link">Merchant Cash Advance</a>,{' '}
        <a href="/services/business-term-loan" class="link">Business Term Loan</a>, and{' '}
        <a href="/services/invoice-factoring-tool" class="link">Invoice Factoring</a>. Figures are typical. Rates are
        quoted individually.
      </p>
      <p>
        If two products both fit, apply and ask a specialist to lay them out side by side. The posts here will not
        pick for you; they will help you ask better questions before you sign.
      </p>
    </>
  )
}
{
  category === 'Costs & Rates' && (
    <>
      <p>
        A quote is not useful until you can compare it to another quote. Factor rates are not interest rates.
        Equipment should cover its own payment. This category is the arithmetic: how to convert a factor rate into a
        number you can put next to a term loan, and how to test whether a machine will break even before you sign for
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Run the <a href="/resources/loan-calculator" class="link">loan calculator</a> if you want a payment sketch.{' '}
        <a href="/services/merchant-cash-advance" class="link">Merchant cash advances</a> and{' '}
        <a href="/services/collateral-asset-loans" class="link">Collateral &amp; Asset Loans</a> are the products
        those posts most often sit next to. Do not treat a blog example as your rate. Your specialist will show the
        full cost of any offer before you sign.
      </p>
      <p>
        Bring a quote, an invoice, or a factor rate to the conversation. The math is easier when the numbers are
        yours, and the file is still reviewed individually.
      </p>
    </>
  )
}
{
  category === 'Cash Flow & Debt' && (
    <>
      <p>
        Slow seasons and stacked daily payments are cash-flow problems, not always profitability problems. A revolving
        line of credit can cover payroll between deposits. Consolidation of Positions can replace several remittances
        with one. The articles here walk through how to size a seasonal gap and how stacked advances drain an
        account.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the issue is timing, start with a{' '}
        <a href="/services/business-line-of-credit" class="link">Business Line of Credit</a>. If several funders are
        already hitting the account, read{' '}
        <a href="/services/consolidation-of-positions" class="link">Consolidation of Positions</a> and apply with a
        list of open balances. Savings of 30%–50% on daily or weekly payments are an average for Falcon clients; your
        file decides the number.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not take a new stack to paper over an old one without a plan. These guides are for getting that plan
        straight before you apply, not a promise of terms.
      </p>
    </>
  )
}